; discrete_alloc: allocate a discrete model and a discrete sample and use
; the domain {0, ..., k-1}.
;
; No vector allocated a discrete model or sample before this one, which is
; why the domain could be documented two different ways -- a signed centred
; [-k, k-1] in spec/xqvm/ and {0, ..., k-1} in spec/xqsa/DOMAINS.md and the
; xqvm_py docstrings -- without anything failing. QUI-1150 settled it on the
; second reading: k is the number of values the domain holds, not a
; half-width, so a discrete variable is a case index.
;
; k = 3, so the domain is {0, 1, 2} and the sample defaults to 0. The two
; assignments below walk it from the bottom to the top. A write of -3 was
; legal under the centred reading and is out of domain now; SETLINE and
; ADDLINE enforce that, which the setline_discrete_* vectors pin.

PUSH 4           ; size
PUSH 3           ; k
XQMX r0          ; discrete model, domain {0, 1, 2}

PUSH 4           ; size
PUSH 3           ; k
XSMX r1          ; discrete sample, defaults to 0

; a freshly allocated discrete sample reads its default, the bottom of the
; domain rather than a value the domain merely happens to contain
PUSH 0
GETLINE r1
STOW r2
PUSH 0
OUTPUT r2

; an interior case index
PUSH 1
PUSH 1
SETLINE r1
PUSH 1
GETLINE r1
STOW r3
PUSH 1
OUTPUT r3

; and the top of the domain, k-1 rather than k
PUSH 2
PUSH 2
SETLINE r1
PUSH 2
GETLINE r1
STOW r4
PUSH 2
OUTPUT r4

HALT
